# Docker build context for the localnet stack.
#
# Every Rust service in infra/localnet/docker-compose.yml builds with
# `context: '../..'` — this directory — and its Dockerfile does `COPY . .`.
# So without this file, each build ships the whole checkout to the daemon:
# ~71 GB measured on the base checkout, and an operator watched one collector
# build climb past 40 GB before killing it.
#
# The Rust builds need the workspace and nothing else: Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock,
# rust-toolchain.toml, and the 13 workspace members. `cargo chef` reconstructs
# the dependency cache inside the image, so the host's target/ is not just
# unnecessary — shipping it is what makes the context enormous.
#
# `.claude/tools/docker_context.py` guards this file: it asserts the patterns
# marked REQUIRED below are still here, and that the effective context stays
# under an order-of-magnitude ceiling. Run it with `--measure` to see what
# currently survives, and `make docker-context` to check.
#
# One caveat on the pattern syntax, since it is not gitignore's: Docker cleans
# each pattern with filepath.Clean, so a trailing slash is dropped and there
# is no directory-only form. A bare `target` therefore anchors at this
# directory and would miss `bots/maker-bot/target`; `**/target` is what
# catches a tree wherever it sits.

# === Build output (REQUIRED) ===

# The Rust target tree — 12 GB in the base checkout, and the single thing
# cargo-chef exists to avoid needing from the host.
**/target

# === Agent worktrees (REQUIRED) ===

# One full checkout per agent worktree, each with its own target/ and
# node_modules: 57 GB, the largest tree by far and the one a fresh clone is
# least likely to notice. It is kept out of git by .git/info/exclude, which is
# **user-local and uncommitted** — so nothing in a checkout keeps it out of a
# build context, and no committed ignore file mentioned it before this one.
# A build must never consume another branch's checkout in any case.
.claude/worktrees

# === Git metadata (REQUIRED) ===

# 69 MB of object store, and no build script reads it — the one build.rs in
# the workspace (programs/dropset) only expands sBPF assembly. In a worktree
# `.git` is a small file rather than a directory; the pattern covers both.
**/.git

# === Node / pnpm (REQUIRED) ===

# 1.3 GB, hoisted to the repo root by pnpm-workspace.yaml, plus the per-app
# trees. No root-context build runs pnpm: the frontend and the decks run on
# the host, and the explorer image builds from its own local context.
**/node_modules

# Next.js build output, for the frontend and the decks alike.
**/.next

# Generated in full by the frontend's predev/prebuild hooks — brand assets
# copied in, flags mirrored out of node_modules, token and wallet icons
# fetched. Nothing under it is committed (frontend/.gitignore says so).
frontend/public

# === Node / pnpm (the rest) ===

frontend/out
decks/out
**/dist
**/coverage
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.turbo
**/.pnpm-store

# === Secrets and machine-local state ===

# Never let a credential reach a build context: an env file baked into a layer
# stays in the image. The localnet enclave file holds `op://` references
# rather than values, but it is machine-local either way, and in a worktree it
# is a symlink into the base checkout that would not resolve in the image.
**/.env
**/.env.*
infra/localnet/secrets.local.env

# Machine-local runtime state (see .gitignore for what each one holds).
.audit-loop
.maker-bot

# === Presentation and asset trees ===

# Neither is a Rust build input. The decks tree is 46 MB and the brand assets
# are consumed by the frontend's copy script on the host, not in an image.
decks
brand-assets

# === Editor and OS litter ===

**/.DS_Store
**/.idea
**/.vscode
**/*.swp
